Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implements EDIT PACKET E8 (CT11 + CT12, traces-to G9). Layer 3 of the
deterministic routing work: S7 made routing happen, S8 makes it
measurable.

acrr.py
  CostWeights (frozen), compute_cost (E3 Eq. 1), compute_cost_floor,
  compute_acrr (Eq. 3). compute_acrr_proxy is RENAMED
  compute_read_amplification: it was never an approximation of ACRR but a
  different and independently useful signal — it needs no floor, so it
  survives failed runs and is cheap enough for the S7 tripwire to
  evaluate per turn.

global_history.py
  Fixes the data gap S8 could not have been built on: since S5,
  _extract_telemetry_from_log discarded the path strings and returned
  only len(read_paths)/len(changed_paths), so the floor was uncomputable
  from the projection. It now also returns changed_paths, sorted, so an
  exported row is byte-identical across runs with the same change-set.
  Four new NULLable columns via the S5 PRAGMA-guarded ALTER pattern.

cli.py
  fa stats --calibration: realized ACRR grouped by recommended_mode (the
  E3 section 7.4b table), successful runs only. ACRR is RECORDED for
  every run and filtered at display (Q22) — filtering at write time
  would destroy the data needed to ask whether failed runs are less
  efficient, and could never be recovered retroactively. Routing outcome
  also mirrored to the blackboard, matching the S3.5 scope_estimate
  precedent, so calibration is observable in-session.

Three plan deviations, each because the plan text predated the tree:

  D-S8-1  The rename is not free. Plan and research note both say
          acrr_proxy has 0 occurrences on main; it has 36 across 4 files
          and is a SHIPPED DB column, because S5 landed after that text
          was written.
  D-S8-2  ALTER TABLE RENAME COLUMN rejected despite sqlite 3.46
          supporting it. This projection lives at a shared ~/.fa path
          that an older fa may still read, so a hard rename is a
          cross-version break. The new column is added and backfilled
          from the old one, which stays in place and populated.
  D-S8-3  Weights could not be fitted against real rows (plan step 4):
          global_history holds 2 rows, both degenerate (exit 2, zero
          telemetry). Rather than invent a fit, they are DERIVED from a
          measured property of the repo and the derivation recorded.

Weight derivation. Confirmed the plan's claim by measurement: the
paper's (1.0, 0.02, 0.5, 1.5) put the file axis at 0.43-2.17% of C on
real S5/S7 change-sets, erasing the axis E3 calls the canonical unit of
redundancy. Median src/*.py is 7234 B ~= 1808 tokens (155 files), so
holding delta=1.5 and requiring a median file's token cost to be half
its file cost gives beta = 0.5*1.5/1808 = 0.000415; gamma = 0.1. Floor
axis shares then land at file 9-59% / tokens 29-89% — every axis
material. Replicating E3 section 7.5 on our numbers, a wasteful run
ranked worse than a lean one in 4000/4000 random weightings: the
ordering is robust, so the constants are configurable rather than
sacred.

The floor excludes latency (deterministic C_min, as the paper does in
7.7) and is SELF-REFERENTIAL: it derives from the run's own change-set,
so a run that changed the WRONG files still scores well. ACRR measures
redundancy, never correctness. Recorded here and carried into ADR-16 at
S6.

Tests: +36 in test_e3_cost_model.py (C0 arithmetic, C1 real sqlite and
real export path, C2 AST wiring, C3 path-escape). 13 mutants applied,
13 killed, no survivors — including removing the workspace-escape
guard, reverting the data-gap fix, unclamping suppression, dropping the
migration and the backfill, and restoring the paper's beta.

Suite 3484p/7f: +36 tests, zero regressions. The 7 are the same
pre-existing environment failures as the baseline (absent
semgrep/vulture/pyrefly binaries, two doc tests referencing an
unrelated archived S8 doc) — verified identical with these changes
stashed. ruff, mypy strict, pyrefly and pylint 10.00/10 clean on all
changed files; the 4 contract scripts and the authoring gate pass with
0 diagnostics.
