From 8445d13b67b41ecf2f202136b3dcf92f8e1d202a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: First-Agent <agent@first-agent.dev>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 17:56:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] docs(s10): merge two adversarial reviews into rev 3 of
 the E3 spec

Two reviewers worked the rev 1 draft independently. Neither is a superset
of the other, so this merges the base that moves decisions forward with
the specific findings the other one got right.

Base is the review that resolved open questions instead of annotating
them. Q24 in particular: the loop carries two independent bounds, the
level cap l<3 and the budget k<K, so from level 1 at most two expansions
can ever occur. K=3 is therefore an inert knob and K=1 provably
under-recovers the paper's deceptive class, which needs 1->3. K=2 is the
only value that is both meaningful and sufficient. Q25 gains a
paper-side argument -- r-hat modulates verification effort only (4.3),
no E3 policy ever withholds an action, and LLM-Case is prompt-only, so
withholding write tools would exceed the paper rather than implement it.
That base also reads the -Expand ablation row correctly: 85.1% of 121 is
103 tasks, so the "cheaper" 14.88 mean is taken after dropping the 18
most expensive tasks it failed. It is cheap because it fails, which
makes the ablation stronger than the raw table looks.

Merged in from the second review, which caught a real error:

Gap 14 was written off in rev 1 as "pure token optimisation; no
correctness value". That was wrong. Section 4.4 says E3 "does not
restart from scratch", reusing remembered search hits, and names the
result progressive context expansion -- "the opposite of
maximum-context-first". Reuse is what makes expansion progressive; an
Expand stage that re-discovers context at each level rebuilds the
maximum-context-first failure mode inside the very mechanism meant to
avoid it. The cache stays deferred to keep the slice small, but the
invariant it protects now ships: ExpansionState already carries
observed_read_paths, and a new exit criterion forbids a higher level
re-reading what a lower level read. A deferral became a pinned
guarantee.

Also merged: difficulty_to_level becomes a named, unit-tested function
rather than an inline l <- d-hat, because Algorithm 1 line 2 identifies
a difficulty estimate with a 3-valued scope level by fiat and we should
not inherit that conflation silently. And the framing that s-hat is
unoperationalized by the paper itself -- we are not conforming to a
fully-specified design, we are specifying what the paper left
aspirational, which is an argument against investing in s-hat at all.

Removed rather than merged: front-matter metadata (journal of record,
page and figure counts, code-release URL, simulation seed) that does not
appear in the arXiv HTML we actually fetched. Plausible, but this
document seeds ADR-16 and unsourced claims should not ride in on it. A
sourcing note records the omission so it can be restored from the PDF.

